# Lockfile Proxy Stripping

Local installs may route through a private npm proxy/registry (`~/.npmrc` or
`bunfig.toml`). Bun records the full tarball URL for any registry other than
npmjs.org, so the proxy URL gets baked into every package entry of `bun.lock`.
That host-specific URL must never be pushed: Vercel builds with `bun install`
(`installCommand` in `vercel.ts`) and CI with `bun install --frozen-lockfile`,
and neither can reach the proxy.

This repo is Bun-only: `bun.lock` is the only lockfile. Don't add a
`package-lock.json`.

## Before Committing or Opening a PR

1. **Always** strip proxy URLs from `bun.lock` after any `bun add`,
   `bun remove`, `bun update`, or `bun install` that changed it.
2. Do **not** match on a specific host name. Normalize **any** registry
   tarball URL.
3. Stage the cleaned `bun.lock` together with the `package.json` change that
   produced it.

## What to Strip

Each `bun.lock` package entry is an array of the form:

```
"<key>": ["<name>@<version>", "<resolution>", { ...deps }, "<integrity>"]
```

The **second element** is the resolution/proxy field. When it points at a
registry tarball it must be reset to an empty string (the value Bun uses for
the default registry). Local installs then still go through whichever
registry is configured.

- With proxy:

```
"readdirp/picomatch": ["picomatch@2.3.2", "https://proxy.npm.your-company.com/picomatch/-/picomatch-2.3.2.tgz", {}, "sha512-V7+vQEJ06Z+c5tSye8S+nHUfI51xoXIXjHQ99cQtKUkQqqO1kO/KCJUfZXuB47h/YBlDhah2H3hdUGXn8ie0oA=="]
```

- After stripping:

```
"readdirp/picomatch": ["picomatch@2.3.2", "", {}, "sha512-V7+vQEJ06Z+c5tSye8S+nHUfI51xoXIXjHQ99cQtKUkQqqO1kO/KCJUfZXuB47h/YBlDhah2H3hdUGXn8ie0oA=="]
```

## How to Strip

Use the project's TypeScript script (run by Bun). It is host-agnostic: it
matches any `http(s)` registry tarball URL (`.../-/<name>-<version>.tgz`) and
only rewrites that URL. Git, GitHub, file, and already-clean resolutions are
left untouched.

```bash
bun run strip-lock-proxy
```

The logic lives in
[`scripts/strip-bun-lock-proxy.ts`](../../scripts/strip-bun-lock-proxy.ts).

Verify nothing remains before committing. This exits non-zero if any proxy URL
is still present, so it doubles as a pre-PR gate:

```bash
bun run strip-lock-proxy:check
bun install --frozen-lockfile   # bun.lock still matches package.json
```

## Automated Safety Nets

- **postinstall**: [`scripts/postinstall.ts`](../../scripts/postinstall.ts)
  runs the strip after every `bun install`, `bun add`, `bun remove`, and
  `bun update`, so the `bun.lock` on disk never carries proxy URLs. It also
  unsets a leftover `core.hooksPath=.githooks` from when the repo had its own
  git hooks.
- **Tests / CI**: `test/lockfile.test.ts` fails in `bun test` (and so in CI)
  if `bun.lock` contains a non-npmjs tarball URL.

Vercel deploys on push without waiting for CI. If a hand-edited or rebased
`bun.lock` still has proxy URLs, run `bun run strip-lock-proxy` and commit.

## Diagnosing a Failed Vercel Install

- **`ENOTFOUND`, `ECONNREFUSED`, or timeouts on a non-npmjs host**: proxy URLs
  were pushed. Run `bun run strip-lock-proxy:check`, then
  `bun run strip-lock-proxy`, and commit the cleaned `bun.lock`.
- **Lockfile out of date / frozen-lockfile errors in CI**: `package.json`
  changed without `bun.lock`. Run `bun install`, strip, and commit both
  together.

## Rules

- **Never commit or push `bun.lock` with proxy URLs.**
- Keep the strip logic host-agnostic. Do not hardcode a proxy hostname.
- Only the resolution field is changed. Names, versions, dependency maps, and
  integrity hashes are never modified.
- Do not hand-edit `bun.lock` beyond what `scripts/strip-bun-lock-proxy.ts`
  does.
